    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* token sequence.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a few complete experts; different tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, large MoE models (e.g., DeepSeek-V3, Mixtral-class architectures, Switch-style transformers) combine both: attention and dense layers use TP, while the MoE feed-forward blocks use EP. The combination is sometimes called **TP+EP hybrid parallelism**, and it is typically layered on top of **data parallelism (DP)** for throughput when the model fits with replication.\n\nThe key design tension: TP requires frequent, latency-sensitive collectives on the critical path of *every* layer, while EP requires high-volume, bursty all-to-all communication only at MoE boundaries. Choosing shard sizes, expert placement, and collective algorithms determines whether you are compute-bound or communication-bound.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 What is sharded\n\nFor a transformer layer, the standard Megatron-style sharding for a world size `T`:\n\n**Attention block:**\n- Query, Key, Value projection weights `[d_model, 3 * d_model]` are column-parallel: each rank holds `[d_model, 3 * d_model / T]`.\n- Output projection `W_O` is row-parallel: each rank holds `[d_model / T, d_model]`.\n- Multi-head attention splits naturally along heads; each rank computes `num_heads",
      "reasoning": "",
